g example gdscript I used here was named ```parse_script.gd```\r\n\r\nWhen using a xml documentation file generated using the ```--doctool``` and ```--gdscript-docs```, the generated xml doc file cannot be written to disc. Instead it gives an output like this:\r\n```Checking for errors in the XML class reference...\r\nGenerating the RST class reference...\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"C:\\Users\\Me\\Godot\\make_rst.py\", line 2638, in <module>\r\n    main()\r\n  File \"C:\\Users\\Me\\Godot\\make_rst.py\", line 814, in main\r\n    make_rst_class(class_def, state, args.dry_run, args.output)\r\n  File \"C:\\Users\\Me\\Godot\\make_rst.py\", line 913, in make_rst_class\r\n    with open(\r\n         ^^^^^\r\nOSError: [Errno 22] Invalid argument: 'C:\\\\Users\\\\Me\\\\Godot\\docoutputtest\\\\classes\\\\class_\"parse_script.gd\".rst'\r\n```\r\n\r\nMore importantly, it appears that when using ```--dry-run``` with ```make_rst.py```, no errors are found.\r\n\r\nUpon further inspection this appears to be fixed when editing the xml doc file's ```<class name=\"&quot;parse_script.gd&quot;\" ...``` to remove the ```&quot;```s.\r\n\r\nMost likely this is due to the fact that whenever is used to determine the file name of the .rst file uses the ```class```'s ```name``` attribute directly, instead of filtering it for characters impossible to use in filenames.\r\n\r\nInstead of fixing this directly I'm making this a issue because:\r\n1. I'm not confident enough in my skills to directly make a pr for a large project's doc generation script like this.\r\n2. I'm not sure if this would break an expected naming or other formatting conventions expected with Godot's .rst doc formatting.","closed_by":{"login":"akien-mga","id":4701338,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ3MDEzMzg=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4701338?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/akien-mga","html_url":"https://github.com/akien-mga","followers_url":"https://api.github.co